<%#
  The status badge, in one place: `render({ partial: "shared/status-badge",
  locals: { status } })`. Every page that shows a story or RFC status calls
  this rather than writing `<span class="badge s-...">` itself — which is the
  duplication ringo carries today (`webhook/story.go:656`, and the same markup
  again on the RFC pages).

  ── THE RULE: a story status shown is an EFFECTIVE status ──────────────────

  There are two statuses a story could be described by. The AUTHORED one is
  the `status` column, what the story's markdown says. The EFFECTIVE one is
  `effectiveStoryStatus(rfcStatus, story.status)`
  (`app/models/concerns/effective-status.ts`): the same value, except that a
  `ready` story under a non-active RFC reads `draft`, because the ready queue
  will not offer it.

  Every page in this application shows the EFFECTIVE one. `/rfc/<id>`,
  `/backlog`, `/story/<id>` and the `/rfcs` progress bar all pass a status
  through `effectiveStoryStatus` before it reaches this partial or
  `shared/_status-state`.

  Why that one and not the authored one: a status badge is read as a claim
  about what will happen to the story next, and the thing that decides what
  happens next is `Story.claimable()`, which ranks the effective status. A row
  labelled `ready` that the queue will never hand out is the badge lying, and
  259 rows were doing exactly that before trailmap#17. The authored status is
  not lost — it is in the story's markdown, which `/story/<id>` renders below
  the pill — and it is not a second vocabulary to display, because
  reactivating the RFC restores it with no edit anywhere.

  This is one rule rather than four page-local decisions on purpose. Each page
  was individually a faithful port — ringo's RFC page reads its index
  (effective) while its backlog page reads story rows (authored) — and porting
  the seam faithfully still ships a database that answers one question four
  ways. trailmap is meant to be the one home for this domain, so the seam is
  closed here. It is the same argument `app/models/concerns/backlog.ts` makes
  for the Icebox label: the badge a row wears and the tab that would collect
  it must not be able to disagree.

  Where it does NOT apply, and why: an RFC's own status (`/rfcs` rows, the RFC
  show page's pill, an RFC dependency) has no override — nothing above an RFC
  overrides it — so it reaches here as stored. And `rfcStoryCounts`, the
  done/total headline, counts the authored status; that is not an exception,
  because the only status the override rewrites is `ready`→`draft` and both
  sides of that are `open` there. The bar is different: it has a `ready`
  segment and a `draft` segment, so the override moves a story between them
  and it must.

  ── ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  `status` is whatever the record holds, which is why the unknown arm exists:
  ringo renders `s-unknown` for a dep whose RFC is missing
  (`webhook/story.go:551,684`), and a null column reaches here the same way.

  The status normalisation below sits in the template because `app/helpers` is
  inert in the vendored trails: a controller's `_helpers` never reached the
  view's scope chain. That was filed from here and is fixed upstream in
  trails#7558; trailmap still pins `7cece02d`, so it lands here with the pin
  bump — `bump-vendored-trails-for-testcase-request-bodies` (RFC 0136), which
  moves this line onto `ApplicationHelper`. The partial stays either way.
%>
<% const shown = String(status ?? "").trim() || "unknown"; %>
<span class="badge s-<%= shown %>"><%= shown %></span>
